<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Build a survey result</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Make up the results of a survey of a class of \({@N@}\) learners about two subjects, Geography (\(G\)) and Business Studies (\(B\)). The results must have \(n(G) = {@a@}\), \(n(G \cup B) = {@u@}\), and more learners taking Business Studies than Geography. Many answers are correct.</p><p>Give the number of learners in each region.</p><p>\(G\) only: [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>both: [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p><p>\(B\) only: [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p><p>neither: [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]]</p><p>[[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Two regions are fixed by the facts. \(B\) only \(= n(G \cup B) - n(G) = {@u@} - {@a@} = {@bo@}\), and neither \(= {@N@} - {@u@} = {@nn@}\).</p><p>The other two make \(n(G) = {@a@}\): \(G\) only \(+\) both \(= {@a@}\). For \(n(B) > n(G)\) we need both \(+ {@bo@} > {@a@}\), so both must be at least \({@lo@}\). So both can be any whole number from \({@lo@}\) to \({@a@}\).</p><p>For example: \(G\) only \({@la@}\), both \({@lb@}\), \(B\) only \({@bo@}\), neither \({@nn@}\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
